=== FINDINGS FROM CHUNK 6/9 (Artifact 2 part 1) ===
1. [MATHEMATICAL_APPENDIX.md](/Users/georgejackson/Developer_Projects/Constraint_Engineering/MATHEMATICAL_APPENDIX.md), `§2 Class-Specific Diversity Discount` and later `§2` subsections

**FIND:** `q` is used with opposite meanings in the same appendix. In `§0.1`, `q_i` is a baseline failure probability. In `§2`, `q_ik = d_ik · p_ik` is an effective detection probability. That notation collision makes later formulas ambiguous and invites invalid substitutions across sections.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<<
> q_ik = d_ik · p_ik
====
> p^{eff}_{ik} = d_{ik} · p_{ik}
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** This rename has to propagate through every later `§2` formula that currently uses `q_ik` or `q_ik^dec`, including the `f_del`, `η_dec`, and `φ_fmt` subsections. It also affects `§6`, because the machine-side term should reference `p^{eff}_{M,i,k}`, not a second, contradictory meaning of `q`.

2. [MATHEMATICAL_APPENDIX.md](/Users/georgejackson/Developer_Projects/Constraint_Engineering/MATHEMATICAL_APPENDIX.md), `§6 Combined Machine-HIL Detection Model`, `Combined Detection Formula`

**FIND:** `C_M(k)` is defined as a per-class machine detection term, but the text says it “= F_n”, which is false. `F_n` is the weighted sum over classes, not a per-class quantity. The same line also drops the earlier `§2` extensions by reverting to scalar `d_{M,i}` and raw `p_{M,i,k}`.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<<
- C_M(k) = 1 − Π_{i=1}^{n_M} (1 − d_{M,i} · p_{M,i,k}) — machine cumulative detection (= F_n)
====
- p^{eff}_{M,i,k} — machine-side effective detection probability for class k after the §2 adjustments; when no extra adjustment applies, p^{eff}_{M,i,k} = d_{M,i,k} · p_{M,i,k}
- C_M(k) = 1 − Π_{i=1}^{n_M} (1 − p^{eff}_{M,i,k}) — per-class machine cumulative detection
- F_n = Σ_{k=1}^{K} w_k · C_M(k) — weighted machine-only structured coverage
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** This changes every downstream use of `C_M(k)`. Any implementation or prose that treats `C_M(k)` as identical to `F_n` is wrong and must be updated. It also means the machine side of `G_n` should inherit `§2` factors such as delivery feasibility, decomposition penalty, and parser yield when those are active.

3. [MATHEMATICAL_APPENDIX.md](/Users/georgejackson/Developer_Projects/Constraint_Engineering/MATHEMATICAL_APPENDIX.md), `§6 Combined Machine-HIL Detection Model`, `Reduction Properties`

**FIND:** The row claiming `K=1, d=1, uniform p -> C(n)` is incomplete and false as written. `G_n` only collapses to simple corroboration if the human-machine priming term is also zero and the single class has unit weight. With `ρ_MH > 0`, the model does not reduce to `C(n)`.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<<
| K=1, d=1, uniform p | C(n) | Simple corroboration model |
====
| K = 1, w_1 = 1, d = 1, uniform p, ρ_MH = 0 | C(n_M + n_H) = 1 − (1 − p)^{n_M + n_H} | Simple corroboration model under full human-machine independence |
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** The sentence immediately below the table, “Every simpler model in the white paper and this appendix is a special case of G_n,” remains defensible only with these extra assumptions stated explicitly. Without them, the reduction claim overstates what `G_n` actually contains.

4. [MATHEMATICAL_APPENDIX.md](/Users/georgejackson/Developer_Projects/Constraint_Engineering/MATHEMATICAL_APPENDIX.md), `§2 Decomposition Yield Bounds`

**FIND:** `q_ik^dec = η_dec(i) · d_ik · p_ik` can exceed `1`. The text explicitly allows `η_dec(i) > 1`, so this formula can produce impossible probabilities whenever `η_dec(i) > 1 / (d_ik p_ik)`.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<<
> q_ik^dec = η_dec(i) · d_ik · p_ik
====
> p^{eff,dec}_{ik} = min(1, η_dec(i) · d_{ik} · p_{ik})
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** The later sentence saying the orchestrator should maximise `f_del · η_dec` can no longer be treated as a probability rule. It is only a yield heuristic unless it is similarly clipped or reframed as expected findings rather than detection probability.

5. [MATHEMATICAL_APPENDIX.md](/Users/georgejackson/Developer_Projects/Constraint_Engineering/MATHEMATICAL_APPENDIX.md), `§0.1 Corroboration Branching C(n)`

**FIND:** The stated “boundedness constraint” is wrong. In the normalised Ising/Boltzmann form already given, state probabilities are non-negative for any finite `ψ_ij`, because the Bernoulli base term is non-negative, the exponential term is strictly positive, and `Z > 0`. The inequality is not needed to ensure non-negativity.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<<
**Boundedness constraint:** The coupling constants must satisfy Σψ_ij ≤ −Σlog(1 − q_i) to ensure all state probabilities remain non-negative. (Verified by SymPy, March 2026.)
====
**Normalisation note:** No additional inequality constraint on the pairwise couplings is required for non-negativity. For any finite ψ_ij, the Bernoulli base measure is non-negative, exp(Σ_{i<j} ψ_ij x_i x_j) is strictly positive, and the partition function Z is positive, so P(x) is automatically non-negative and normalised.
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** Any later audit note or proof claim that relies on that inequality as a required domain condition should be removed or rewritten as an optional regularisation choice. No downstream formula in this chunk actually needs the bound.

6. [MATHEMATICAL_APPENDIX.md](/Users/georgejackson/Developer_Projects/Constraint_Engineering/MATHEMATICAL_APPENDIX.md), `§6 Combined Machine-HIL Detection Model`, `Numerical Illustration`

**FIND:** The scenario table is numerically inconsistent with the stated parameters. Using `p_M=0.3`, `d_M=0.7`, `n_M=3` gives `C_M = 1 - (1 - 0.21)^3 = 0.507`, which matches. But using `E=0.85`, `M=0.9`, `α=0.4`, `d_H=0.9`, `n_H=2` gives `p_H = 0.85·(0.4 + 0.6·0.9) = 0.799`, so `C_H = 1 - (1 - 0.9·0.799)^2 = 0.921`, not `0.698`. The `ρ=0.3` and `ρ=0.6` combined values are also wrong from the stated formula.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<<
| Scenario | Detection |
|---|---|
| Machine only (C_M) | 0.507 |
| Human only (C_H) | 0.698 |
| Combined, ρ = 0 (fully independent) | 0.961 |
| Combined, ρ = 0.3 (mild priming) | 0.851 |
| Combined, ρ = 0.6 (significant priming) | 0.748 |
| Combined, ρ = 1.0 (fully correlated) | 0.507 |
====
| Scenario | Detection |
|---|---|
| Machine only (C_M) | 0.507 |
| Human only (C_H) | 0.921 |
| Combined, ρ = 0 (fully independent) | 0.961 |
| Combined, ρ = 0.3 (mild priming) | 0.825 |
| Combined, ρ = 0.6 (significant priming) | 0.689 |
| Combined, ρ = 1.0 (fully correlated) | 0.507 |
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** Any prose elsewhere that cites `0.698`, `0.851`, or `0.748` as support for the priming discussion must be updated. The methodology-formality table below this one remains consistent with the stated `f(E,M)` formula and does not need change.

=== FINDINGS FROM CHUNK 7/9 (Artifact 2 part 2) ===
Scope: chunk 7 only. I found 4 HARD defects.

1. **Section 7.3, Ascending Abstraction: the inequality is attached to the wrong quantity**

**FIND:** `Y(t)` is defined as cumulative yield: `Y(t) = N(t) · H̄(t)`. The text then says  
`(dH̄/dt)/H̄(t) > |dλ/dt|/λ(t)` is the condition under which `dY/dt > 0`.  
That is not correct. This inequality is the condition for the **instantaneous value-generation rate** `y(t) = λ(t) · H̄(t)` to increase. It is not the condition for cumulative `Y(t)` to increase.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<< (old)
Total yield Y(t) increases despite the rate decrease when the relative depth increase exceeds the relative rate decrease:

> **(dH̄/dt) / H̄(t) > |dλ/dt| / λ(t)**

This is the formal condition for ascending abstraction. When this inequality holds, dY/dt > 0 despite declining finding rate. This captures creative deepening as a distinct cognitive mode from analytical exhaustion.
====
Define the instantaneous value-generation rate as:

> **y(t) = λ(t) · H̄(t)**

The value-generation rate increases despite the declining finding rate when the relative depth increase exceeds the relative rate decrease:

> **(dH̄/dt) / H̄(t) > |dλ/dt| / λ(t)**

This is the formal condition for ascending abstraction in rate terms. When this inequality holds, dy/dt > 0 even though dλ/dt < 0. The cumulative yield Y(t) = N(t) · H̄(t) is non-decreasing whenever findings carry non-negative H(x); the inequality above applies to y(t), not to Y(t). This captures creative deepening as a distinct cognitive mode from analytical exhaustion.
>>>>
```

```text
<<<< (old)
Ascending abstraction (§7.3) holds when the finding rate is decreasing but the relative depth increase exceeds the relative rate decrease, guaranteeing that total yield Y(t) is still increasing.
====
Ascending abstraction (§7.3) holds when the finding rate is decreasing but the relative depth increase exceeds the relative rate decrease, guaranteeing that the instantaneous value-generation rate y(t) = λ(t) · H̄(t) is still increasing.
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** This changes the interpretation of the §7.4 stop guard, but not its boolean structure. Any chart, implementation note, or benchmark text that currently describes this inequality as a condition on `Y(t)` should be relabeled as a condition on `y(t)`.

2. **Section 7.4, Online Total Value Estimator: `λ(t)` and `k_decay(t)` are contradicted**

**FIND:** The prose first defines `k_decay(t)` as the local exponential decay rate and explicitly distinguishes it from Duane `λ(t)`. The next sentence then says `λ(t)` is the empirical decay rate estimated from consecutive round values. Those two statements cannot both be true.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<< (old)
Here k_decay(t) is the local exponential decay rate of the sliding-window generation rate v_w(t), estimated from consecutive round values. This is distinct from the Duane NHPP intensity λ(t) in §7.1, which is the global power-law intensity and is always positive.

v_w(t) is the sliding-window smoothed generation rate. λ(t) is the empirical decay rate estimated from consecutive round values.
====
Here v_w(t) is the sliding-window smoothed generation rate. k_decay(t) is the local exponential decay rate estimated from consecutive round values. This is distinct from the Duane NHPP intensity λ(t) in §7.1, which is the global power-law intensity and remains strictly positive.
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** This must propagate to any implementation or figure labels tied to §7.4. The estimator should use `k_decay(t)` locally; `λ(t)` should remain reserved for the Duane intensity from §7.1. Otherwise later comparisons between §7.1 and §7.4 become symbolically ambiguous.

3. **Section 7.5, Objective Alignment: the denominator and empty-set rule are internally inconsistent**

**FIND:** `O_A` is defined as `|verified findings in F_conv| / |F_conv|`, but the later guard says `O_A` is only meaningful when there are at least 2 **verifiable** findings, and the limitation says it is computed only from the subset SymPy can verify. Those statements conflict. Also, the convention `if F_conv = ∅, O_A = 1` conflicts with the later mutual-suppression rule that sets `O_A = ⊥` when `F_conv = ∅` and suppression triggers.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<< (old)
> **O_A = |verified findings in F_conv| / |F_conv|**
>
> Convention: if F_conv = ∅, O_A = 1
====
Let **V_conv = {f ∈ F_conv : verifiable(f)}**.

> **O_A = |{f ∈ V_conv : verified(f)}| / |V_conv|**
>
> **O_A is defined iff |V_conv| ≥ 2**
>
> **If F_conv = ∅ and M_suppress does not trigger, set O_A = 1 by convention.**
>
> **If 0 < |F_conv| and |V_conv| < 2, set O_A = ⊥ and use S_sync = Δ̄.**
>>>>
```

```text
<<<< (old)
When F_conv = ∅ and M_suppress triggers, O_A = ⊥ and S_sync = ⊥ — evaluate mutual suppression instead.
====
When F_conv = ∅ and M_suppress triggers, this overrides the empty-set convention: O_A = ⊥ and S_sync = ⊥. Evaluate mutual suppression instead.
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** This changes the branch order for any implementation of `S_sync`: `mutual suppression override` must be checked before the empty-set convention. It also makes the stated limitation true, because unverifiable findings no longer silently depress `O_A`.

4. **Section 7.12, FFF model: `ν` is overloaded and no longer means the same thing as in §7.1**

**FIND:** In §7.1, `ν` is the **re-injection coefficient** in  
`λ_ext(n) = λ_Duane(n) + ν · Δ_{n−1}`.  
In §7.12, the same symbol appears in  
`D_{n+1} = ν · D_n + ε_n`,  
where it acts as a **net retention/contraction factor** for the residual defect stock. Those are different mechanisms with different meanings. The cross-reference “`ν` is the re-injection rate (§7.1)” is therefore false.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<< (old)
> **D_{n+1} = ν · D_n + ε_n**

Where ν ∈ [0, 1) is the Re-injection Coefficient (§7.1) and ε_n ≥ 0 represents novel defects surfaced by consequence analysis.
====
> **D_{n+1} = κ · D_n + ε_n**

Where κ ∈ [0, 1) is the FFF net retention factor: the fraction of unresolved defects that remain after one find-fix-follow cycle, after accounting for fixes and any defects reintroduced by those fixes. ε_n ≥ 0 represents novel defects surfaced by consequence analysis.
>>>>
```

```text
<<<< (old)
> **ε_n < D_n · (1 − ν)**

That is, the novel defects from consequence analysis must be fewer than the net defects resolved. When ν < 1 and this condition holds, |D_n| decreases monotonically.
====
> **ε_n < D_n · (1 − κ)**

That is, the novel defects from consequence analysis must be fewer than the net defect-reduction margin left after one cycle. When κ < 1 and this condition holds, D_n decreases monotonically.
>>>>
```

```text
<<<< (old)
> **D* = ε* / (1 − ν)**
====
> **D* = ε* / (1 − κ)**
>>>>
```

```text
<<<< (old)
> **n_{1/2} = −ln(2) / ln(ν)**

At ν = 0.5, half-life is 1 round. At ν = 0.8, half-life is 3.1 rounds.
====
> **n_{1/2} = −ln(2) / ln(κ)**

At κ = 0.5, half-life is 1 round. At κ = 0.8, half-life is 3.1 rounds.
>>>>
```

```text
<<<< (old)
**Relationship to Duane γ (§7.1):** For large n, the Duane intensity ratio λ(n+1)/λ(n) ≈ 1 − γ/n. The FFF contraction rate ν maps to this: γ > 0 ↔ ν < 1 (contractive), γ = 0 ↔ ν = 1 (churn), γ < 0 ↔ ν > 1 (divergent).
====
**Relationship to Duane γ (§7.1):** For large n, the Duane intensity ratio λ(n+1)/λ(n) ≈ 1 − γ/n. The FFF net retention factor κ gives the same regime classification: γ > 0 ↔ κ < 1 (contractive), γ = 0 ↔ κ = 1 (churn), γ < 0 ↔ κ > 1 (divergent). κ is not the same parameter as the re-injection coefficient ν in §7.1; ν contributes to κ but does not equal it in general.
>>>>
```

```text
<<<< (old)
When ν = 0 (no re-injection), convergence is immediate upon resolution.
====
When κ = 0 (full resolution with no retained defects), convergence is immediate upon resolution.
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** Every later use of the FFF state-transition coefficient in §7.12 must be renamed to `κ`. This also reveals a remaining modelling dependency: if the document wants a formal bridge from §7.1 reinjection `ν` to FFF retention `κ`, that bridge needs its own equation rather than a shared symbol.

=== FINDINGS FROM CHUNK 8/9 (Artifact 2 part 3) ===
Using section headings as locations because this chunk has no line numbers.

1. **FIND:** In `§8.2`, the first emergence test uses `σ̂(Y)` even though the hypothesis is about a difference: composite yield minus the best individual yield. That is statistically under-specified and can misclassify emergence, because uncertainty belongs to the comparison statistic, not to a generic `Y` term.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<<
**Emergence condition:**

> **Y_composite(t) > max{Y_i(t)} + z_conf · σ̂(Y)**

Where σ̂(Y) is the bootstrap standard error of the Y estimates and z_conf is a confidence multiplier (z_conf = 1.96 for 95% confidence). The strict inequality Y_composite > max(Y_i) is necessary but not sufficient — it must exceed the measurement uncertainty to distinguish genuine emergence from statistical noise.
====
**Emergence contrast:**

> **Δ_emerge(t) = Y_composite(t) - max_i Y_i(t)**

**Emergence condition:**

> **Δ_emerge(t) > z_conf · σ̂(Δ_emerge)**

Where σ̂(Δ_emerge) is the bootstrap standard error of the emergence contrast and z_conf is a confidence multiplier. The raw inequality Y_composite(t) > max_i Y_i(t) is necessary but not sufficient; the contrast must exceed its own measurement uncertainty to distinguish genuine emergence from statistical noise.
>>>>
```

```text
<<<<
| Composite Y > individual max Y + z_conf·σ̂ | Bench test, all conditions | Y_composite ≤ max(Y_i) + 1.96·σ̂(Y) on majority of tasks |
====
| Emergence contrast is positive beyond uncertainty | Bench test, all conditions | Δ_emerge ≤ z_conf·σ̂(Δ_emerge) on majority of tasks |
>>>>
```

```text
<<<<
| σ̂(Y) | Bootstrap standard error of Y estimates | This appendix §8.2 |
====
| σ̂(Δ_emerge) | Bootstrap standard error of the emergence contrast Y_composite - max_i Y_i | This appendix §8.2 |
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** This change must also propagate to the strong-emergence test and to the notation summary. If `σ̂(Y)` is left in those downstream references, the section will still be testing the wrong uncertainty object.

2. **FIND:** In `§8.2`, `Y_union(t) = |⋃ F_i(t)| · H̄_union(t)` is presented as the aggregation baseline, but `F_i(t)` is not restricted to blind, pre-interaction outputs. That breaks the control: after confer, `F_i(t)` can already contain interaction-generated findings. The same problem appears in `§8.5`, where “genuine, not aggregation” is tested with `Δ` and `O_A`, which diagnose groupthink, not aggregation surplus.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<<
The union of individual outputs would give:

> Y_union(t) = |⋃ F_i(t)| · H̄_union(t)

**Strong emergence condition:** Y_composite(t) > Y_union(t) + z_conf · σ̂(Y). This establishes that interaction produced cognitive value beyond what the union of independent outputs would yield.
====
The non-interactive aggregation baseline is the union of blind outputs collected before confer:

> Y_union^blind = |⋃ F_i^blind| · H̄_union^blind

**Strong emergence contrast:** Δ_strong(t) = Y_composite(t) - Y_union^blind

**Strong emergence condition:** Δ_strong(t) > z_conf · σ̂(Δ_strong). This establishes that interaction produced cognitive value beyond what simple aggregation of blind outputs would yield.
>>>>
```

```text
<<<<
| Emergence is genuine, not aggregation | Δ and O_A joint analysis | High Δ with low O_A across majority of tasks |
====
| Emergence is genuine, not aggregation | Strong-emergence test against blind union | Δ_strong ≤ z_conf·σ̂(Δ_strong) on majority of tasks |
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** After this fix, `Δ` and `O_A` still matter, but only for the later “groupthink vs independence” diagnostic table. They should no longer be treated as the primary falsification test for aggregation. The empirical-evidence paragraph in `§8.2` also needs to be read as illustrative unless it is explicitly tied to `Y_union^blind`.

3. **FIND:** In `§8.1`, the metacognitive control signals are not fully computable. `Δ(r)` is called an “independence measure from §7.6”, but `§7.6` defines directional pairwise adoption/drop metrics, not a single per-model round scalar. Also, `v̄(r) = verified findings / total findings` is undefined when a round produces zero findings.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<<
- **Verification rate:** v̄(r) = verified findings / total findings at round r
- **Adoption delta:** Δ(r) = independence measure from §7.6
====
- **Verification rate:** v̄_i(r) = verified_i(r) / total_i(r) when total_i(r) > 0; otherwise v̄_i(r) = NA, and the verification-rate adjustment is skipped for that model in that round.
- **Adoption delta:** Δ_i(r) = (1/(n-1)) Σ_{j≠i} Δ̄(A_i→A_j; r), the round-r mean of the directional adoption-delta terms defined in §7.6.
>>>>
```

```text
<<<<
| Δ > threshold | Excessive deference | Reassert independent analysis before engaging with confer input |
====
| Δ_i(r) > threshold | Excessive deference | Reassert independent analysis before engaging with confer input |
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** This fix propagates to every later use of plain `Δ` in this section. In particular, “moderate `Δ` with high `O_A`” in `§8.2` should now be read as the aggregated per-model scalar `Δ_i(r)`, not an undefined generic symbol. NA rounds also need to be logged explicitly rather than silently treated as low verification.

4. **FIND:** In `§8.3`, the text says “The CDSFL composite system meets criteria 1–3 by construction.” That contradicts `§8.1`, which explicitly says whether models actually respond to metacognitive feedback is an empirical question. Criterion 3 is real behavioural adjustment, not merely the existence of a protocol.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<<
The CDSFL composite system meets criteria 1–3 by construction. Criterion 4 (measurable improvement from metacognitive adjustment) is a testable empirical claim. If confirmed, the system qualifies as second-order cognitive under this definition.
====
The CDSFL composite system meets criteria 1–2 by construction. Criterion 3 is operationally specified by the metacognitive feedback protocol but must be empirically demonstrated in behaviour, and criterion 4 tests whether that adjustment improves performance. If criteria 3–4 are confirmed, the system qualifies as second-order cognitive under this definition.
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** This reveals a gap in `§8.5`: the current falsifiable-claims table tests improvement after feedback, but it does not separately test whether the prescribed behavioural adjustment happened at all. Criteria 3 and 4 are currently conflated.

5. **FIND:** In the `§8.2` empirical-evidence paragraph, “The composite system was measurably more capable than any pair” is stronger than the evidence actually given. The stated evidence is only that Gemini found 16 issues Claude Opus and Codex missed. That supports added value from a third perspective, but it does not establish the formal claim against every pairwise baseline.

**FIX:**
```text
<<<<
**Empirical evidence:** Three-architecture adversarial review (March 2026). Gemini found 16 issues that Claude Opus and Codex missed across 8 rounds of mutual review. These were structural findings visible only from a different analytical perspective. The composite system was measurably more capable than any pair.
====
**Empirical evidence:** Three-architecture adversarial review (March 2026). In one 8-round mutual-review sequence, Gemini surfaced 16 issues that were not present in the combined outputs of Claude Opus and Codex at the point they were found. This is consistent with the hypothesis that a third analytical perspective added non-trivial value, but it is illustrative rather than dispositive until the formal emergence contrasts above are computed against pairwise baselines.
>>>>
```

**FOLLOW:** This brings the narrative evidence back into line with the formal tests in `§8.2` and `§8.5`. FOLLOW: No downstream impact identified beyond preventing the evidence paragraph from overstating what has been shown.

